Hi Alex,

I worked a bit to implement the "Tiny RNN" (https://github.com/cgc/tinyRNN)
- but by now I couldn't find a way as the codes provided are not super
generalizable and are really specific to their datasets... I will try some
new attempts during the week but not sure what I can get out of it.

Meanwhile, I asked Ayelet to provide us with some data from another task.
This is the task shown here
https://www.nature.com/articles/s41593-021-00839-z by Peter Murphy. In a
nutshell, this is also an evidence accumulation task but with hidden
changes in the environmental state.  The evidence samples were small
checkerboard patches presented in a rapid sequence at different locations
along a semicircle in the lower visual hemifield (number of samples is
variable between 2 and 12 samples). Sample positions were generated from
one of two noisy sources. The =E2=80=98active=E2=80=99 source was chosen ra=
ndomly at the
beginning of each trial and could change after each sample, with a
probability (hazard rate *H*) of 0.08 in the main experiment (The change is
what is referred as "Change point"). The participant=E2=80=99s task was to =
report
which source was active at the end of each sequence via left- or
right-handed button press.

In these file we have these variables:

samp =3D sequence of samples (in degrees). Negative - left hemifield,
positive - right hemifield.
choices: 0 or 1; 0 - left, 1 - right
dist_lr=3D which distribution is active at each sample (0 - left, 1 - right=
)
cp =3D 1, if change point occurred.
correct_trials =3D 1 - correct, 0 - incorrect
LLRin =3D log-likelihood ratio of each sample in the sequence (quantifies t=
he
strength of support for one distribution

over the other)

nsamps =3D how many samples in the sequence (max 12, min 2)


Maybe we can also check how the disRNN works with this!


Ale




